Formatter: format any screen, and terminal state without content

Muster replays a pane's terminal into a fresh one when a surface attaches
(docs/mip/0003-own-daemon.md, section 5). The pinned C formatter reaches
only the active screen, and always emits content with the terminal's
extras. So it cannot reproduce the primary screen while a program holds
the alternate one, and cannot emit tabstops, the scrolling region or the
pen apart from content. formatter.zig's own comment on TerminalFormatter
names the way out: a no-content terminal formatter followed by screen
formatters. This exposes both to C:

- ghostty_formatter_terminal_state_new: the terminal formatter with
  content none. Same options; the selection must be NULL.
- ghostty_formatter_screen_new: a screen formatter for the primary or
  alternate screen, looked up on every format call. It can omit content,
  and can emit the blank rows below the last row with text, which the
  formatter always drops, so that its output spans every row.
- formatter.Options.unstyled_blanks, default false, which only
  ghostty_formatter_screen_new turns on. The formatter writes a run of
  blank cells before the next text in whatever style the previous text
  left open, so a background or underline bleeds across the gap; with
  the option it closes the style first. Default output is unchanged, and
  a test pins both.

Additive only: two functions, one struct, one option that defaults to
the existing output, no existing behavior changed.
Muster carries this for good rather than sending it upstream, so it is
kept small enough to rebase on a re-pin. The Zig tests at the end of
src/terminal/c/formatter.zig cover it: zig build test-lib-vt.

